      "The fate of the Tonquin became one of the most famous stories of the Pacific fur trade. Most of what is known comes from an interpreter from the Quinault nation who survived and told the story later. The disaster ended Astor's plans to dominate the trade on the northwest coast, and for decades few European ships ventured into Clayoquot Sound.",
      "The protests of 1993, known as the War in the Woods, began after the British Columbia government decided to allow logging in two-thirds of Clayoquot Sound. Protesters gathered at the Kennedy River bridge every morning to block the logging trucks, and the arrests drew international attention. The government later created a scientific panel that recommended more careful logging, and much of the sound's old-growth forest now stands protected.",
      "Tofino's rainforest is home to giant western red cedars, some more than a thousand years old, and Sitka spruces that rise more than 70 m. The forest floor is thick with moss and ferns, and the trees drip with rain for much of the year. Meares Island's Big Tree Trail leads visitors to some of the largest cedars in the world.",
      "Winter storms along the outer coast are so spectacular that storm watching has become a tourist season of its own. Waves 6 m high or more come crashing against the rocks at Cox Bay and Chesterman Beach, and visitors gather in hotels with big windows to watch. Tofino's surf is best in the stormy months, when the swells roll in from the open Pacific.",
